{"name":"Treasure Isle","hex":"#609d91","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"treasure","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A collection of valuable things; accumulated wealth; a stock of money, jewels, etc.","Anything greatly valued."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To consider to be precious; to value highly.","To store or stow in a safe place."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/treasure"},{"term":"isle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An island, usually very small. Compare with islet."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Obsolete spelling of aisle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/isle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Uninhabited island","blocks":[{"text":"An uninhabited island, desert island, or deserted island, is an island, islet, or atoll which lacks permanent human population. Uninhabited islands are often depicted in films or stories about shipwrecked people, and are also used as stereotypes for the idea of \"paradise\". Some uninhabited islands are protected as nature reserves, and some are privately owned. Devon Island in Canada's far north is the largest uninhabited island in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Desertisland.jpg/330px-Desertisland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uninhabited_island","wikidata":"Q1065592"},{"title":"Buried treasure","blocks":[{"text":"Buried treasure is a literary trope commonly associated with depictions of pirates, alongside Vikings, criminals, and outlaws in the Old West. According to popular conception, these people often buried their stolen fortunes in remote places, intending to return to them later."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Pyle_pirates_burying2.jpg/330px-Pyle_pirates_burying2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buri